        <div class="fc-delta-reason">New forecast. The Islamabad MOU is a 14-point framework without specified HEU limits, verification protocols, IAEA access arrangements, sanctions relief schedules, or permanent Hormuz governance terms. Resolving all elements within 60 days requires both sides to accept constraints that domestic constituencies oppose: Iran's IRGC-aligned hardliners reject HEU limits; US/Israeli pressure demands intrusive verification. Historical precedent (JCPOA: 20 months) suggests 60 days is highly compressed. Base case: talks proceed but framework remains partial or deadline slips. A complete signed agreement covering both HEU and permanent Hormuz terms is a tail-positive outcome.</div>
        <dl class="fc-meta-row">
          <dt>Resolution</dt><dd>A signed document — confirmed by TIER1 and acknowledged by both governments — must specify: (1) explicit HEU production or stockpile limits; AND (2) a permanent or long-term Hormuz governance framework. Both elements required. MOU renewal without these specifics = NO.</dd>
          <dt>Sources OK</dt><dd>TIER1 reporting citing the full text or official government summaries. State media without independent TIER1 corroboration is insufficient.</dd>
          <dt>Ambiguity</dt><dd>If a document is signed but one element (HEU OR Hormuz governance) is 'in principle' or 'to be negotiated,' resolve AMBIGUOUS-AS-NO.</dd>
        </dl>
        <div class="fc-indicators">
          <span class="fc-indicator state-CONTRADICTED">MOU specifies explicit HEU production limits — CONTRADICTED (Fars News: current MOU lacks HEU specifics)</span>
          <span class="fc-indicator state-NOT_OBSERVED">IAEA access framework under formal negotiation — NOT OBSERVED</span>
        </div>
