  <!-- ========== 3. WHAT MOVED (Last 9 days, Jun 11-19) ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Jun 11–19, 2026)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive · banned-verb lint enforced · each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">
      <p>The Islamabad Memorandum — a 14-point document brokered by Pakistani Prime Minister Shehbaz Sharif — was signed electronically by President Trump and President Pezeshkian approximately June 14–17 and formally acknowledged at a ceremony in Burgenstock, Switzerland on June 19. Witkoff and Araghchi led their respective delegations; Vice President Vance and Iranian parliamentary speaker Ghalibaf also attended. The agreement establishes a 60-day ceasefire on all fronts, the reopening of the Strait of Hormuz to commercial shipping, and a 60-day window for structured nuclear negotiations. → moved <span class="cite">F2</span></p>

      <p>The June 16 horizon for <span class="cite">F1</span> closed without a qualifying convened session. Iran's June 1 suspension of negotiations remained in force without formal rescission before that date. The sixth round, scheduled for June 15, was cancelled following Israeli strikes on June 13. Al Jazeera's June 16 reporting stated that nuclear talks would "begin after framework deal signing," referring forward to the June 19 ceremony. The electronic relay of the MOU text prior to June 16 constituted a written-message procedure rather than a convened session under the resolution criteria. → moved <span class="cite">F1</span></p>

      <p>Israel and Iran exchanged direct strikes on June 7–8 — Israeli aircraft struck targets in Tehran, Isfahan, Karaj, and Tabriz while the IRGC launched ballistic missiles against Ramat David Air Base — but this exchange occurred before the June 10 window opening. On June 14, IDF renewed strikes in the Beirut area, prompting Iranian preparations for a response; the United States intervened to prevent the Iranian launch, leaving the June 14 action one-directional. No bilateral direct exchange within the June 10–24 window was confirmed by TIER1 sources. → moved <span class="cite">F3</span></p>

      <p>Commercial shipping in the Strait of Hormuz remained at 5–10% of pre-war levels as of June 19. Kpler tracking estimated approximately 26 transits on June 17 against a pre-war baseline near 94. Shipping executives cited by CNBC on June 18 stated the backlog "may take weeks" to clear even after MOU signing. The US military's own MCM timeline holds at 40–50 days for initial clearance, and P&amp;I war-risk insurance had not been reinstated at commercial scale as of the most recent reporting. → moved <span class="cite">F4</span></p>

      <p>The Knesset dissolution bill's first reading passed 106-0 on June 2, with coalition whip Ofir Katz stating the election date would be inserted before the final two readings. As of June 19, no second or third reading has been scheduled and no fixed election date has been agreed between Netanyahu (October preference) and haredi coalition partners Shas and UTJ (September preference). The coalition is without a functioning legislative majority for the first time since its formation, according to i24News, and all coalition legislation has been withdrawn from the Knesset floor. → moved <span class="cite">F6</span>, <span class="cite">F7</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 4. SCENARIO MAP ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="scenario-map">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Scenario Map
      <span class="meta">Computed from Forecast Board · 1 sentence per scenario</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="scenario-map">
      <div class="prob-bar-container">
        <div class="prob-bar prob-escalation" style="width:20%;">Escalation 20%</div>
        <div class="prob-bar prob-status" style="width:50%;">Protracted 50%</div>
        <div class="prob-bar prob-deescalation" style="width:30%;">De-esc 30%</div>
      </div>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-esc">Escalation path:</strong> A single IDF strike against Iranian or IRGC targets during the 60-day ceasefire window — or an Iranian MFA statement conditioning the MOU on the Lebanon clause — would constitute an arguable breach and return both parties to active military exchange. The MOU lacks binding verification and Hezbollah is not a signatory, leaving friction points that either side could invoke. This scenario has contracted sharply from pre-MOU levels but remains the primary tail risk.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-prot">Protracted path:</strong> The MOU framework stalls in verification disputes, sanctions sequencing arguments, and domestic political constraints without formally breaking down. Hormuz transit recovers partially (above 10 but below 50 transits per day) as insurance markets wait for certified clearance. Nuclear talks convene but produce no signed framework within the 60-day window. Israel's political transition (<span class="cite">F6</span>, <span class="cite">F7</span>) proceeds in parallel without direct linkage to the diplomatic track. This is the modal scenario.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-desc">De-escalation path:</strong> The 60-day structured negotiations produce a partial nuclear framework, IRGC issues a formal transit clearance notice enabling commercial insurance reinstatement, and Hormuz transits ramp toward pre-war levels within 45 days. The Netanyahu government's transition to caretaker status reduces Israeli military activism. Both <span class="cite">N2</span> and <span class="cite">N3</span> close YES. This scenario requires domestic hardliner constraints in Tehran to be overridden at the leadership level — a non-trivial condition given Khamenei's June 18 public statement.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 5. INSIDE IRAN ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · 200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>Supreme Leader Khamenei — communicating through written statements via his son Mojtaba, who holds signatory authority — released a statement on June 18 asserting that the MOU "does not mean Iran accepts the American viewpoint." IRNA framed the Hormuz-reopening clause as a concession Iran extracted from Washington rather than one Iran made. State-controlled Tasnim outlined Iran's three conditions for the 60-day talks: the lifting of all sanctions, the cessation of Israeli operations in Lebanon, and the unfreezing of $300 billion in oil revenues. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>

      <p>Fars News carried a contrasting line, noting that the MOU does not include explicit HEU limits and that Iran's enrichment program at Fordow and Natanz "continues to operate." Kayhan, the hardliner daily, published front pages on June 16 and June 18 describing the framework as a "temporary technical arrangement" and cautioning against any agreement that constrains Iran's "legitimate defense capacities." These outlets reflect IRGC-aligned sentiment absent from the Burgenstock delegation. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>

      <p>IRIB television ran continuous coverage of the June 19 ceremony cast as a "diplomatic victory" for Iran, with anchors emphasizing that Iran negotiated from a position of strength. No Iranian state outlet referenced the economic costs of 112 days of Hormuz closure — estimated by the IMF at $15–20 billion in foregone oil revenues — nor did any coverage characterize the June 7–8 military exchange as an adverse outcome for Iran. The gap between IRIB's triumphalist framing and the hardliner press's skepticism about MOU terms captures the principal domestic tension that will determine whether the 60-day negotiating framework holds. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 6. REGIME CHANGE WATCH ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="regime-change">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Regime Change Watch
      <span class="meta">12-month horizon · weekly review only (Sundays) · no intra-week deltas</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="regime-watch">
      <div class="regime-watch-meta">Last reviewed: <strong>2026-06-10</strong> · Next review: <strong>2026-06-21</strong></div>

      <div class="regime-row">
        <span class="name">🇮🇷 Iran (Regime Collapse)</span>
        <span class="p">20–30%</span>
        <div class="drivers">The MOU signing creates a short-term legitimacy buffer for the Pezeshkian government, with IRIB framing the agreement as a diplomatic victory. However, Khamenei's parallel statement distancing himself from US terms, combined with IRGC-aligned media skepticism, maintains the underlying tension between reform-adjacent diplomatic engagement and hardliner institutional resistance. The 112-day Hormuz closure's economic costs ($15–20 billion in foregone oil revenues per IMF) have not been publicly acknowledged. Regime stability range unchanged pending Sunday 2026-06-21 review.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="regime-row">
        <span class="name">🇮🇱 Israel (Netanyahu Steps Down)</span>
        <span class="p">40–50%</span>
        <div class="drivers">The 106-0 first reading of the dissolution bill represents the coalition formally driving toward its own end. The coalition lacks a functioning legislative majority, the haredi partnership is fractured over the election date, and the Degel HaTorah revolt has removed reliable legislative support. An electoral transition now appears ordered rather than traumatic — which paradoxically reduces the probability of sudden involuntary collapse while accelerating formal dissolution. Range unchanged pending Sunday 2026-06-21 review.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

